\section{The tests}{

All the tests are tests of a composite hypothesis: the increments
\eqn{\Delta x_1, \dots, \Delta x_n} are i.i.d. with a distribution of the family of
the model, whose parameters are unknown and estimated on the sample.

\strong{Gamma process (\code{"HGP"})}, increments \eqn{\Gamma(a \Delta, b)}:
\itemize{
\item \code{"HME1"}: Henze, Meintanis and Ebner (2012), Laplace transform
(statistic \code{gofgamma::HME1}, tuning parameter 1);
\item \code{"BE"}: Betsch and Ebner (2019), characterization by a fixed point
(statistic \code{gofgamma::BE}, tuning parameter 1);
\item \code{"VGE"}: test of Villasenor and Gonzalez-Estrada (2015), ratio of two
variance estimators (function \code{goft::gamma_test}), with a p-value by
parametric bootstrap;
\item \code{"KS"}, \code{"CvM"}, \code{"AD"}, \code{"Watson"}: Kolmogorov-Smirnov,
Cramer-von Mises, Anderson-Darling and Watson (\code{gofgamma}).
}

For the tests HME1, BE, KS, CvM, AD and Watson, the statistic and the
parametric bootstrap are those of the package \code{gofgamma} (the shape is
estimated by \code{gofgamma::gamma_est}, and each bootstrap sample of the
gamma distribution with this shape is estimated again); all the bootstrap
statistics are kept, which gives both the p-value and the critical value.

\strong{Wiener process (\code{"HWP"})}, increments
\eqn{\mathcal{N}(\mu \Delta, \sigma^2 \Delta)}: tests of normality with estimated
mean and variance.
\itemize{
\item \code{"AD"}, \code{"CvM"} and \code{"Lilliefors"}: Anderson-Darling, Cramer-von
Mises and Lilliefors tests (package \code{nortest}). The Lilliefors test is the
Kolmogorov-Smirnov test with estimated mean and variance: \code{"KS"} is also
accepted for it, and the result is labelled \code{"Lilliefors"};
\item \code{"SW"}: Shapiro-Wilk (\code{stats::shapiro.test});
\item \code{"JB"}: Jarque-Bera (\code{tseries::jarque.bera.test});
\item \code{"DAgostinoPearson"}: D'Agostino-Pearson omnibus test \eqn{K^2},
combining skewness and kurtosis (\code{fBasics::dagoTest}).
}

\strong{Inverse Gaussian process (\code{"InvGaus"})}, increments
\eqn{IG(\mu \Delta, \eta \Delta^2)}:
\itemize{
\item \code{"ABEV1"}, \code{"ABEV2"}: Allison, Betsch, Ebner and Visagie (2022)
(\code{gofIG}, tuning parameter 10, maximum likelihood), characterization of
the distribution function (see below);
\item \code{"HK1"}, \code{"HK2"}: Henze and Klar (2002) (\code{gofIG}), empirical Laplace
transform (see below);
\item \code{"KS"}, \code{"CvM"}, \code{"AD"}: Kolmogorov-Smirnov, Cramer-von Mises and
Anderson-Darling (\code{gofIG});
\item \code{"VGE_ratio"}: Villasenor and Gonzalez-Estrada (2015), ratio of two
variance estimators, asymptotic p-value, suited to large samples
(\code{goft::ig_test}, method \code{"ratio"});
\item \code{"VGE_gamma"}: Villasenor and Gonzalez-Estrada (2015), transformation of
the data to gamma variables of shape 1/2 and Anderson-Darling test
(\code{goft::ig_test}, method \code{"transf"}).
}

\strong{Tests ABEV1, ABEV2, HK1 and HK2.} Let \eqn{X_1, \dots, X_n} be the
sample, \eqn{IG(m, \lambda)} the inverse Gaussian distribution of mean \eqn{m} and
shape \eqn{\lambda}, \eqn{\hat m = \bar X} and
\eqn{\hat\lambda = 1 / \overline{(1/X - 1/\bar X)}} the maximum likelihood
estimators. The four tests use the scaled data
\eqn{Y_j = X_j / \hat m} and \eqn{\hat\varphi = \hat\lambda / \hat m}: under
H0, \eqn{Y_j} is close to \eqn{IG(1, \varphi)}, and the distribution of the
statistic only depends on \eqn{\varphi}. Each statistic is a weighted
\eqn{L^2} distance, H0 is rejected for large values, and the p-value is
obtained by parametric bootstrap: \code{B} samples of \eqn{IG(1, \hat\varphi)}
of size \eqn{n}, the statistic being computed again (with new estimates)
on each one. \code{gofIG} gives only the p-value, so the column
\code{critical.value} is \code{NA}.
\itemize{
\item \code{"HK2"} compares the empirical Laplace transform
\eqn{L_n(t) = \frac{1}{n} \sum_j e^{-t Y_j}} with the fitted one
\eqn{L_{\hat\varphi}(t) = \exp(\hat\varphi (1 - \sqrt{1 + 2t/\hat\varphi}))}:
\deqn{HK2 = n \int_0^\infty (L_n(t) - L_{\hat\varphi}(t))^2\, dt.}
\item \code{"HK1"} uses the differential equation
\eqn{L(t) + \sqrt{1 + 2t/\varphi}\, L'(t) = 0}, which characterizes the
Laplace transform of \eqn{IG(1, \varphi)}; the statistic measures how
far \eqn{L_n} is from satisfying it:
\deqn{HK1 \propto n \int_0^\infty \left(L_n(t) + \sqrt{1 + 2t/\hat\varphi}\, L_n'(t)\right)^2 e^{-a t}\, dt,}
with \eqn{a = 0}.
\item \code{"ABEV1"} and \code{"ABEV2"} use a characterization of the distribution
function: with the score
\eqn{\tau(y) = -f'(y)/f(y) = \frac{1}{2}(\varphi + 3/y - \varphi/y^2)} of
the density \eqn{f} of \eqn{IG(1, \varphi)}, a positive variable
\eqn{Y} has this distribution if and only if
\eqn{F(t) = E[\tau(Y) \min(Y, t)]} for all \eqn{t > 0}. With
\eqn{F_n} the empirical distribution function,
\deqn{ABEV = n \int_0^\infty \left(\frac{1}{n} \sum_j \hat\tau(Y_j) \min(Y_j, t) - F_n(t)\right)^2 w(t)\, dt,}
with the weight \eqn{w(t) = e^{-a t}} for ABEV1 and
\eqn{w(t) = e^{-a t^2}} for ABEV2, and \eqn{a = 10}.
}

The four statistics are double sums over the sample, computed with R
loops: their cost grows as \eqn{n^2}, times \eqn{B + 1} for the bootstrap.
For \eqn{n = 300} and \code{B = 200}, one test takes about 8 seconds for HK2,
2 minutes for ABEV1 and HK1, and 7 minutes for ABEV2. With many
increments (for instance the method \code{"ExtBridge"} of \code{\link[=gof.levy]{gof.levy()}}), prefer
HK2 or the other tests.

\strong{Variance-gamma (\code{"VG"}) and Meixner (\code{"Meixner"}) processes}:
\code{"KS"}, \code{"CvM"}, \code{"AD"} and \code{"Watson"}, the Kolmogorov-Smirnov,
Cramer-von Mises, Anderson-Darling and Watson statistics (Stephens,
1986). The parameters are
estimated by maximum likelihood with the package \code{levyfit} (functions
\code{\link[levyfit:vg_mle]{levyfit::vg_mle()}} and \code{\link[levyfit:meixner_mle]{levyfit::meixner_mle()}}); the distribution function is computed by
numerical integration of the density, and the p-value is obtained by
parametric bootstrap (Stute, Gonzalez Manteiga and Presedo Quindimil,
1993). With a small sample, \code{\link[levyfit:meixner_mle]{levyfit::meixner_mle()}} may give no estimate
(see the section Algorithm of its help): the test is then not computed,
with this message.
}

